Glide renders into the frontend's context (doc 12 §5).

qemu-3dfx's Glide device is a dispatcher: at grGlideInit it dlopens a
host-side wrapper (libglide2x) and looks up 183 gr/gu entry points in it.
Upstream then hands the wrapper a window handle and the wrapper makes a GL
context on it -- which the player cannot do: QEMU is a library inside it and
the frame has to arrive as a texture, not as pixels on a window.

This reverses the handshake for a wrapper that opts in. A new
QemuFxUiOps::glide_host_ops returns the GlideHostOps table in
glidept/glide_host.h (begin/present/end/get_proc); init_glide2x passes it to
the wrapper's optional `setHostOps` export right after loading it, before
anything can open a window with it. A wrapper without the symbol, or a
frontend that returns NULL (every display but the embed one), is upstream
unchanged -- ui/sdl2.c registers no glide_host_ops and keeps its window.

The search for the library is the other half, and it is the same one
d3dpt_exec_load.c does: QEMU_GLIDE_LIB, then build/glide/libglide2x.so, then
the loader's own path, then /usr/local/lib. The build tree deliberately
comes before the loader: a distribution's libglide2x is an OpenGLide without
setHostOps and would try to open a window of its own. Each attempt names
what it loaded, and hDll's NULL check moves above the setConfig lookups,
which dlsym'ed on a NULL handle (RTLD_DEFAULT on glibc: the whole process)
when nothing was found.

